          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do bodies disagree about using a short-acting reliever on its own in asthma?",
          "answer": "Yes, guidance does differ on this point, at least for mild asthma.\n\nThe asthma guidance summary (Global respiratory strategy report and national asthma panel guidance, educational summary) says the preferred reliever in mild asthma is a point of disagreement between authorities:\n\n- **Global strategy guidance** describes an inhaled corticosteroid combined with formoterol as the preferred reliever across steps of treatment.\n- **Some national panel guidance** describes a short-acting bronchodilator reliever, used alongside a regular controller, as still appropriate in mild disease.\n\nSo the two positions are not the same. The first moves away from using a short-acting reliever alone. The second keeps it as an option, but with a regular controller, not on its own. The passage does not say which approach a particular person should use.\n\nThe retrieved text does not cover the reasons behind each position, the specific medicines, or how the choice would apply to any individual. A clinician who manages asthma can explain which approach fits a given situation and why.\n\nIf you or someone you know has asthma and is having trouble breathing, wheezing that is getting worse, or reliever use that is not helping, seek urgent medical care now.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
